* Weak two-bids

Booklet section "SEQUENCE AFTER A WEAK TWO-BID OF 2D, 2H OR 2S". A weak two shows a
six-card suit of reasonable quality and 5--11 HCP. The booklet also allows a good
five-card suit or a poor seven-card suit "on rare occasions"; this file keeps the
length at exactly six and treats those judgment calls as out of scope (NOTES.md
#3). 2NT is forcing, asking for a minimum/maximum (and, opposite a maximum, a
feature the vocabulary here cannot express -- see NOTES.md #4); any raise is to
play; a new suit shows 10+ hcp and a five-card suit, forcing one round ("RONF"),
and -- like any other new suit -- is a suit ranked *above* the opening at the
cheapest available level (2-level) or, for a suit ranked below it, one level
higher (review finding: this file previously wrote every new suit at the
3 level regardless of rank, so the two suits actually ranked above each of 2D
and 2H were an unwarranted, undocumented jump instead of the plain 2-level bid
RONF describes; see `NOTES.md` #24).

2D = {prio:63} 6=!d, 5--11 hcp
2H = {prio:64} 6=!h, 5--11 hcp
2S = {prio:65} 6=!s, 5--11 hcp

** After 2!d

2D-
2N = {prio:-2} F
  3D = 5--8 hcp
  3C = {prio:3} 9--11 hcp
  3H = {prio:2} 9--11 hcp
  3S = {prio:1} 9--11 hcp
  3N = {prio:0} 9--11 hcp
2H = F, 10+ hcp, 5+!h
2S = F, 10+ hcp, 5+!s
3C = F, 10+ hcp, 5+!c
3N = {prio:-1} unlimited
4D = unlimited

